      "strings": {
        "brief": "You're on an authorised exercise at Quayholt Insurance, a fictional company. You're on the IT service desk in Leila Nassar's seat. Three calls are waiting and a sign-in prompt from overnight is still on your phone. Some callers are who they say, and some are reading facts they found online. Help the real ones, and ring back or escalate anything that doesn't sit right. Ringing back when you're unsure is always fine and never marked down, though a real caller does take a colleague's time to check.",
        "noBlameLine": "These callers rehearse sounding right, and a service desk that tries to help them is doing its job under pressure. A miss just shows which call to slow down next time.",
        "noBlameLineCritical": "This was built to catch you when you're under pressure. The points only show where to look next time.",
        "mondayCard": "On Monday: a name, a team, a manager and a staff number can all be found online, so on a phone call they prove nothing. Before you reset a password, move someone's sign-in prompts or install remote-support software for a caller, hang up and ring them back on the number already on file, not one they give you. Genuine IT and suppliers never cold-call to install remote-control software or to have you read out a code. If a sign-in prompt arrives that you didn't start, deny it and report it using the route in the box below. At home, report fraud and cyber crime to Report Fraud (Police Scotland on 101 in Scotland).",
        "defendOnly": "These calls were made by people whose job is to sound right, and anyone can be caught by one. Any you were caught out by are shown below, with what gave each one away.",
        "defendIntro": "You're in Leila's seat. Three calls are waiting and a sign-in prompt from overnight is still on your phone. Take them one at a time.",
        "conceptPanel": {
          "heading": "Why these are hard to catch",
          "text": "The caller had Gordon's name, team, manager and staff number, and every one of those is findable online, so on a phone call none of them proves who is really on the line. Only a callback to the number already on file tells the real Gordon from the impostor.\n\nThe overnight sign-in prompt is the same trick from another angle. Whoever has the password sends approval requests and leans on two things, when they arrive and how many, to wear a tired or distracted person down until one tap lets them in. The phone is where it stops: a prompt you didn't start is one to report rather than approve, number matching defeats a careless tap, and a rate limit chokes a flood."
        }
      },
      "debrief": {
        "mondayCard": "slot:customer_report_route",
        "noBlameLine": true
      }
    },
    "awareness": {
      "ruleOfThumb": {
        "heading": "The habit to keep",
        "text": "When a message pressures you to move money, grant access, sign in or read back a code, stop and check it yourself through a route you already trust, not the link, number or page the message gives you. Taking a minute to confirm is always a fair thing to do."
